Sleeperstride

Ingredients: ELECTRO SWING + ETHEREAL, DREAMY & AIRY + Brass  
Creative Catalyst: It emerged from night trains where insomniac commuters jam with pocket synths.

Sleeperstride is the narcotic, slow-motion evaporation of big-band stride jazz into the twilight gloom of trans-continental railway corridors. It strips electro swing of its cartoonish, frantic 128 BPM bounce, instead dragging 1930s swing syncopation down to a weightless, half-time drift where felt-dampened brass instruments breathe like sleeping bodies, and gritty, unquantized pocket-operator percussion clatters in perfect lockstep with steel tracks sliding over switch points.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 68–78 BPM (with deep, unquantized swing quantization). The groove sways laterally rather than stomping forward, mirroring the rhythmic rocking of European sleeper cars taking high-speed turns.
- Harmonic Palette: Diminished jazz passing chords and lush major 9ths melted across modal Dorian and Lydian washes; dusty shell voicings decayed through cassette heads and pitch-drift warble.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Sub-Bell Aero-Mic'ing: Brass bells fitted with vintage Harmon or cup mutes, then recorded inches away with high-gain ribbon microphones to capture lung-hiss and saliva clicks rather than bright projection.
  2. Kinetic Rail-Doppler Resynthesis: An impossible technique where vintage gramophone horn samples are fed into an algorithmic waveguide that calculates the Doppler shift and cabin-glass resonant flutter of a train decelerating from 140 km/h to an unmanned junction halt.
  3. Pocket Bit-Erosion: The juxtaposition of crunchy, 12-bit handheld sampler swing hits (micro-chopped 1920s stride piano) swimming inside vast, pristine 32-bit floating-point diffuse hall reverbs.
- Dynamics: Anti-climactic and vaporous. Tracks avoid aggressive EDM builds and drop-hooks; instead, they exhale into sustained horn suspensions, hollow bass hums, and delicate micro-percussion that slowly evaporates into room-tone tape hiss.

Scene & Origin Lore
Sleeperstride was born between 02:00 and 05:00 on the trans-Alpine night trains threading Vienna, Zurich, and Ljubljana. Insomniac night-shift commuters, exiled brass players with travel-size pocket trumpets, and software nomads armed with pocket samplers (Teenage Engineering PO-33s and Dirtywave M8s) began gathering in the narrow carpeted corridors of couchette cars to escape the silence of restless berths. The subculture adopted a uniform of practical night-travel elegance: voluminous vintage wool coats with interior pockets tailor-widened for miniature hardware, silk sleeping masks propped casually as headbands, and train-ticket lapel pins denoting specific late-night lines. Their ritual is the "Berth Handshake"—the silent passing of 3.5mm auxiliary splitters and thermos flasks of cardamon-laced chicory coffee in the vestibule while conductor boots crunch on track ballast outside.

Typical Instruments: Muted flugelhorn, felt-wrapped pocket cornet, valve trombone, handheld pocket samplers/synths, detuned stride piano samples, bowed contrabass, binaural railway carriage contact mics.

For Fans Of: Bohren & der Club of Gore, The Caretaker, Parov Stelar (slowed down 800%), Grouper.

